sections.s2_technical:
Short polling is the simplest pull: the client requests updates on a fixed interval, say every five seconds. It is trivial to build and works everywhere, but it wastes requests when nothing changed and still adds latency up to the polling interval when something does change.

```java
// Short polling: client asks repeatedly, mostly getting "no change"
while (true) {
    Update u = client.get("/transfers/status?since=" + lastSeen);
    if (u != null) handle(u);
    Thread.sleep(5000);   // fixed interval → wasted calls + up-to-5s latency
}
```

Long polling improves on this: the client makes a request and the server holds it open until there is an update or a timeout, then the client immediately reconnects. This gives near-real-time delivery with ordinary HTTP, at the cost of many held connections and reconnect overhead.

Server-Sent Events, or SSE, is a true push over a single long-lived HTTP connection where the server streams events to the client one way. It is simple, works over HTTP, and auto-reconnects, ideal for one-directional feeds like a live transaction ticker. WebSocket is a full-duplex connection where both client and server can send at any time, ideal for interactive, bidirectional use like chat or live trading.

```java
// SSE: server pushes a stream of events one-way over one connection
@GetMapping(value = "/transactions/stream", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
public SseEmitter stream(String accountId) {
    SseEmitter emitter = new SseEmitter();
    registry.register(accountId, emitter);   // server will push when events occur
    return emitter;
}
```

Mobile push notifications are a specialized push delivered through platform services like APNs or FCM, which can reach a device even when the app is closed, used for alerts like a fraud warning. The decision depends on several factors: if updates are rare and latency-tolerant, polling is fine; if you need near-real-time one-way updates, use SSE; if you need bidirectional real-time, use WebSocket; if you must reach a closed app, use push notifications. A senior nuance is connection cost and scale: push connections consume server memory per client and need a presence registry and load-aware fan-out, so for millions of mostly idle clients, occasional push notifications or long-polling can be cheaper than holding a million open WebSockets.

